      "desc": "Facilities, general.",
      "narrative": "On April 7, 2026, a black rat snake was found in Seba\u2019s short-tailed bat enclosure. The black snake was immediately\nremoved. A radiograph of the snake confirmed that 1 bat was consumed. The black rat snake was subsequently\nmicrochipped and released per the institution\u2019s wildlife management protocols. Since snake entry occurred despite the\ncorrective measure taken from previous predation events, the decision was made to move the bat colony out of the\nexhibit. A fabrication company was hired to perform an extensive renovation of the Bat Cave.\nThe enclosure was not maintained in a manner that prevented the entry of an unwanted species resulting in the death of a\nbat. NOTE: At the time of inspection renovations were completed. The bats were moved into the Bat Cave on June 7,\n2026, with no incidence of snake entry since.\nCorrect by ensuring that the Bat Cave is kept in good repair to protect the animals from injury.\nThis inspection and exit briefing were conducted with the facility representative.\nAdditional Inspectors:\nGLORIA MCFADDEN, VETERINARY MEDICAL OFFICER n"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": "3.125(a)  Critical Repeat\n   \nFacilities, general. \nOn April 7, 2026, a black rat snake was found in Seba\u2019s short-tailed bat enclosure. The black snake was immediately \nremoved. A radiograph of the snake confirmed that 1 bat was consumed. The black rat snake was subsequently \nmicrochipped and released per the institution\u2019s wildlife management protocols. Since snake entry occurred despite the \ncorrective measure taken from previous predation events, the decision was made to move the bat colony out of the \nexhibit. A fabrication company was hired to perform an extensive renovation of the Bat Cave. \n \nThe enclosure was not maintained in a manner that prevented the entry of an unwanted species resulting in the death of a \nbat. NOTE: At the time of inspection renovations were completed. The bats were moved into the Bat Cave on June 7, \n2026, with no incidence of snake entry since.  \n \nCorrect by ensuring that the Bat Cave is kept in good repair to protect the animals from injury. \n \nThis inspection and exit briefing were conducted with the facility representative. \n \nAdditional Inspectors: \nGLORIA MCFADDEN, VETERINARY MEDICAL OFFICER n",
